## 1. Project Overview

This project investigates the early outbreak of COVID-19 in Nigeria using data from the first 34 days of the outbreak, covering the period between **20 March 2020 and 22 April 2020**.

The study applies a Discrete-Time Markov Chain (DTMC) framework to represent the movement of individuals between three epidemiological states: Susceptible, Infected, and Recovered (SIR).

Using R programming, the project explores COVID-19 trends, examines the geographical distribution of reported cases, estimates transition probabilities, constructs a transition probability matrix, and investigates the long-run behaviour of the resulting Markov chain.

The project combines exploratory data analysis, statistical modelling, probability theory, and geographical data visualisation to examine the early progression of COVID-19 in Nigeria.

## 2. Project Objectives

The major objectives of this project are:

1. To explore and analyse the early COVID-19 outbreak data in Nigeria.
2. To examine the distribution and trends of confirmed COVID-19 cases over the study period.
3. To visualise the geographical distribution of reported cases across Nigerian states, highlighting locations with available case data.
4. To construct a discrete-time Markov chain representing transitions between Susceptible, Infected, and Recovered states.
5. To estimate transition probabilities and construct the transition probability matrix.
6. To investigate steady-state probabilities and future transition behaviour.
7. To calculate the fundamental matrix and expected absorption times.

## 3. Dataset Description

The analysis uses an Excel dataset containing daily COVID-19 records for the first 34 days of the outbreak in Nigeria.

**Study period:** 20 March 2020 – 22 April 2020.

**Number of daily observations:** 34.

### Dataset variables

| Variable | Description |
|---|---|
| Date | Date of observation |
| Day | Sequential day of observation |
| Confirmed_Nigeria | Cumulative confirmed cases in Nigeria |
| Confirmed_Lagos | Cumulative confirmed cases in Lagos |
| Confirmed_Abuja | Cumulative confirmed cases in Abuja |
| Incidence_Nigeria | Daily new cases in Nigeria |
| Incidence_Lagos | Daily new cases in Lagos |
| Incidence_Abuja | Daily new cases in Abuja |

The dataset was cleaned and transformed before analysis. Date variables were converted into R date format, while numerical variables were converted into appropriate numeric data types.

**Important geographical note:** The dataset provides individual confirmed-case figures for Lagos and Abuja, not all 36 states. The geographical visualisation uses Nigerian state boundaries, but does not imply that state-level case figures were available for every state.

